; --anon must not merge two lots that only a tag tells apart, even when
; their prices agree at the lesser precision.  The typed lot {$1.03}
; carries the tag (lotA); the computed lot from 23 AAPL @@ $23.60 has the
; price $1.026087 and the same date.  Rounded to the typed price's two
; decimals both are $1.03, which is enough for a typed lot price to name a
; computed lot (#524); only the tag keeps these two apart.  --anon drops
; the tag and gives each posting the lot whose annotation is exactly the
; anonymized one, without editing the lot itself
; (lot-anon-price-rendering.test).  Had it found that lot by the rounding
; match of #524 instead, the computed lot would have taken the typed price
; ({B1.03}), in the other order the typed price would have become the
; computed one ({B1.026087}), and the balance would have merged each pair
; into one 33 A lot.

; Typed lot first.
2020/01/01 Buy
    Assets:Broker    10 AAPL {$1.03} [2020/01/01] (lotA)
    Assets:Cash

2020/01/01 Buy more
    Assets:Broker    23 AAPL @@ $23.60
    Assets:Cash

; Computed lot first.
2020/02/01 Buy
    Assets:Broker    23 AAPL @@ $23.60
    Assets:Cash

2020/02/01 Buy more
    Assets:Broker    10 AAPL {$1.03} [2020/02/01] (lotB)
    Assets:Cash

; Without --anon: four lots.
test reg --lots Broker --format "%(amount)\n"
10 AAPL {$1.03} [2020/01/01] (lotA)
23 AAPL {$1.026087} [2020/01/01]
23 AAPL {$1.026087} [2020/02/01]
10 AAPL {$1.03} [2020/02/01] (lotB)
end test

; With --anon: the same four lots, renamed (AAPL to A, $ to B, tags dropped).
test reg --anon --lots Broker --format "%(amount)\n"
10 A {B1.03} [2020/01/01]
23 A {B1.026087} [2020/01/01]
23 A {B1.026087} [2020/02/01]
10 A {B1.03} [2020/02/01]
end test

test bal --anon --lots Broker --format "%(total)\n"
23 A {B1.026087} [2020/01/01]
23 A {B1.026087} [2020/02/01]
10 A {B1.03} [2020/01/01]
10 A {B1.03} [2020/02/01]
end test
